# API Versioning

Every public backend HTTP route is versioned. Prefer a well-engineered, stable contract over ad-hoc paths.

## Path convention

```
/api/v{N}/<resource>[/<id>][/<action>]
```

Examples:

| Method | Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| GET | `/api/v1/health` | Liveness (versioned too) |
| GET | `/api/v1/profile` | Current user/resident profile |
| GET | `/api/v1/profile/{id}` | Profile by id |
| GET | `/api/v1/residents` | List |
| GET | `/api/v1/metrics/evaluation` | Evaluation report |

## Rules

- **Always** include `/api/v{N}` — never ship bare `/profile` or unversioned `/api/residents` for new work.
- Start new public APIs at **`v1`**. Do not invent `v0` routes for production contracts.
- Breaking change (remove/rename field, change meaning, auth, or status codes) → **`v{N+1}`** new routes; keep `vN` working until callers migrate.
- Additive non-breaking fields may stay on the current `vN`.
- Group routers by version in code (e.g. FastAPI `APIRouter(prefix="/api/v1")`).
- Errors: stable JSON shape across versions (`detail` / `code` / `request_id` as the project standardizes).
- Document new/changed routes in the same PR/change set as the code.
- Health may also expose `/health` unversioned for probes **only if** `/api/v1/health` remains the app contract; prefer the versioned one as the documented API.

## Anti-patterns

- Mixing `/api/residents` and `/api/v1/residents` as equals.
- Silent breaking changes on `v1`.
- Version only in headers while paths stay unversioned (path version is the project standard).
